  "metabolism": "Absorbed from the gut, but the liver gets to some of it on the way, so only about 32% of an oral dose reaches the blood. Under 10% sticks to blood proteins. It is broken down either by joining a small molecule straight onto it or by reducing the keto group and then joining one on. The main products are hydromorphone-3-glucuronide, hydromorphone-3-glucoside and dihydroisomorphine-6-glucuronide; smaller amounts of dihydroisomorphine-6-glucoside, dihydromorphine and dihydroisomorphine turn up as well. It is broken down in the liver, and a smaller part leaves the kidneys unchanged. There is no sign that it is broken down in the body by the cytochrome P450 system, and in the lab it only weakly inhibits CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C8, 2D6 and 3A4 (IC50 above 50 micromolar).",  "metabolismSources": ["https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/7686/smpc"]
